Consider the graph below with $5$ vertices and $4$ edges.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.5,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3,2);
\coordinate (D) at (4.5,3.2);
\coordinate (E) at (4.5,0.8);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (A);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Why is this graph NOT a tree?

The network diagram has $5$ vertices $A, B, C, D, E$ with $6$ edges.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,2);
\coordinate (B) at (1.8,3.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.6,2);
\coordinate (D) at (3.6,0);
\coordinate (E) at (0,0);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- (E) -- (A);
\draw (A) -- (C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt) node[left] {$A$};
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt) node[above] {$B$};
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$C$};
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt) node[below left] {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which pair of edges can be removed to leave a valid spanning tree?
